const text: LessonText[][] = [[{"say":"Theme A begins inside the machine: what a computer is made of and how it stores everything as bits.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"The CPU contains the control unit (CU), arithmetic logic unit (ALU) and registers (program counter, MAR, MDR, accumulator). It runs the fetch–decode–execute cycle. GPUs have many simple cores for parallel tasks such as graphics and machine learning."},{"t":"p","text":"Memory hierarchy: registers → cache (L1, L2, L3) → RAM → secondary storage (SSD, HDD). Faster memory is smaller and more expensive. RAM is volatile; ROM and secondary storage are non-volatile."},{"t":"p","text":"Data representation: binary (base 2), hexadecimal (base 16, one hex digit = 4 bits), and characters (ASCII, Unicode). Logic gates (AND, OR, NOT, NAND, NOR, XOR) combine bits; truth tables list every input combination."},{"t":"ex","problem":"Convert binary 1011 0110 to hexadecimal.","steps":["Split into nibbles: 1011 = 11 = B, 0110 = 6"],"answer":"B6"}]},{"say":"Computers rarely work alone. The operating system manages one machine; networks connect many.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"An operating system manages memory, processes (scheduling), files, devices (drivers), security and the user interface. Interrupts let hardware signal the CPU; polling checks devices repeatedly."},{"t":"p","text":"Networks: LAN, WAN, VPN and PAN. Devices include switches, routers and access points. The TCP/IP model has layers: application, transport (TCP reliable, UDP fast), internet (IP addresses, routing) and network access."},{"t":"p","text":"Data travels in packets; packet switching sends them along different routes. Security uses encryption (symmetric and asymmetric), firewalls, and authentication."}]}],[{"say":"Nearly every app stores data in a database. Good design keeps it consistent and easy to query.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"A relational database stores data in tables (relations) of records (rows) and fields (columns). A primary key uniquely identifies each record; a foreign key links to another table's primary key."},{"t":"p","text":"Normalization (1NF, 2NF, 3NF) removes redundancy and update anomalies. SQL queries data: SELECT fields FROM table WHERE condition ORDER BY field; joins combine tables."},{"t":"p","text":"Transactions follow ACID properties: atomicity, consistency, isolation and durability."},{"t":"code","text":"SELECT name, grade\nFROM Students\nWHERE grade >= 90\nORDER BY name;"}]},{"say":"Machine learning lets computers learn patterns from data instead of following hand-written rules.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"Supervised learning trains on labeled data (classification, regression). Unsupervised learning finds structure in unlabeled data (clustering). Reinforcement learning learns by rewards and penalties."},{"t":"p","text":"Data is split into training and test sets. Overfitting means a model memorizes training data and generalizes poorly; underfitting means it's too simple. Neural networks pass inputs through weighted layers."},{"t":"p","text":"Ethics: biased training data produces biased predictions; issues include privacy, transparency (explainability), accountability and environmental cost."},{"t":"tip","text":"For ethics questions, name a specific stakeholder, explain how the system affects them, and suggest a safeguard such as auditing training data or keeping a human in the loop."}]}],[{"say":"Theme B is about solving problems: breaking them down and writing efficient algorithms.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"Computational thinking: decomposition, pattern recognition, abstraction and algorithmic design."},{"t":"p","text":"Searching: linear search is O(n); binary search on sorted data is O(log n). Sorting: bubble sort and selection sort are O(n²). Big-O describes how time grows with input size."},{"t":"p","text":"Recursion: a function calls itself with a smaller input and needs a base case. Tracing with trace tables checks how variables change."},{"t":"ex","problem":"How many comparisons, at most, does binary search need for 1,024 sorted items?","steps":["Each step halves the list: 2¹⁰ = 1,024"],"answer":"10 comparisons (plus one final check)"}]},{"say":"Large programs are organized into objects, and data is stored in structures chosen for the task.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"OOP concepts: a class is a blueprint; an object is an instance. Encapsulation hides data behind methods; inheritance lets subclasses reuse and extend a parent class; polymorphism lets one method call behave differently by object type. UML class diagrams show classes, attributes and methods."},{"t":"p","text":"Abstract data types: a stack is LIFO (push, pop); a queue is FIFO (enqueue, dequeue); a linked list connects nodes with pointers; a binary search tree keeps smaller values left and larger right; hash tables give near O(1) lookup."}]}]];
export default text;
